  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSurface analysis shows a quasi-stationary front stretching from\nWest Virginia to Louisville, KY, and roughly parallel to I-40 \nthrough West Tennessee. Abundant moisture persists along this \nboundary, with dewpoints in the low to mid-70s and PWATs above the\n90th percentile (99th percentile north). Radar reveals a broad \nrain shield from north-central Arkansas into the Missouri \nBootheel, where 1.5 to 6 inches of rain fell over the past 12-18 \nhours.\n\nAloft, a shortwave trough over Missouri and weak perturbations in\nArkansas will pivot east over the next 24 hours, bringing rounds\nof scattered convection. Moderately unstable air (MLCAPE\n~2000 J/kg) along and south of I-40 combined with ~20 kt bulk\nshear will primarily support single-cell and loosely organized\nclusters capable of damaging winds. If the wave ejects faster by\nlate afternoon, a linear mode could evolve across NW Tennessee,\nincreasing severe probabilities. Overall, a Marginal Risk (1/5)\nfor Severe Thunderstorms and Marginal Risk (1/4) for Excessive\nRainfall remain across most of the Mid-South. Strong microbursts\nremain the main threat given 7-8 C/km low-level lapse rates\ndespite poor mid-level lapse rates (~5.5 C/km).\n\nScattered convection will become more numerous and potentially\nlinear east of the Mississippi this evening as upper lift\nincreases. A secondary wave late tonight will bring another round\nof showers and storms. While high PWATs and parallel flow favor\nstorm training, low instability and dry antecedent conditions\nwill keep flood potential low.\n\nGuidance continues trending drier for Saturday as shortwave\nenergy moves northeast, with lingering chance PoPs mainly near\nthe Tennessee River through early afternoon.\n\nHigh agreement among model guidance indicates a subtropical ridge\namplifying over the Lower Mississippi Valley through midweek.\nThis will bring another stretch of excessive heat and potential\nheadlines from Sunday through Wednesday, with a medium chance of\nrecord high temperatures early next week. A pattern change\nremains beyond the 7-day forecast.\n\nAC3",
